{"reference":"2 Corinthians 5","book":"2 Corinthians","chapter":5,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter joins three themes without supplying a detailed system for any one of them: all people appear before Messiah’s judgment seat for embodied deeds; life in Messiah is described as new creation; and reconciliation is attributed to God’s action in Messiah and entrusted as a message and ministry. The passage therefore holds accountability, renewal, and reconciliation together without spelling out the full mechanics of final judgment or every dimension of “new creation.”","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 5:10","2 Corinthians 5:17","2 Corinthians 5:18-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_ce19edad0fcaef6dbde652a4"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Images of dwelling and life","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The “earthly tent,” heavenly building, clothing, nakedness, and mortality being swallowed by life are overlapping images. They express longing for life from God while resisting a simple wish to be without a body; Paul says he wants to be further clothed. These verses have generated different accounts of what happens between death and resurrection, but they do not provide an explicit chronological chart. The movement then turns to aiming to please the Lord and to universal appearance before Messiah’s judgment seat.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 5:1-5","2 Corinthians 5:6-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_d5fec637038a9b3591bd3598"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Compressed and debated phrases","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"“Knowing according to the flesh” in verse 16 most likely concerns a way of evaluating people, rather than denying Jesus’ historical embodiment. Likewise, “new creation” in verse 17 can be read personally, communally, as a changed mode of perception and relation, or as the beginning of creation’s renewal; the phrase should not be reduced too quickly to private self-improvement or disembodied escape. Verse 21 is also compressed: its claim that God made the sinless one “to be sin” has been read in sacrificial, representative, participatory, and exchange terms, with no one later atonement system exhausting the wording.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 5:1-10","2 Corinthians 5:16","2 Corinthians 5:20","2 Corinthians 5:21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_427f56adeb8a09b9fce54ffd"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Appeal, representation, and discernment","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"“Walk by faith, not by sight” does not require ignoring observable evidence. In the same chapter, reconciliation begins with God’s initiative and is communicated through commissioned representatives. Calling them ambassadors marks an authorized appeal, but it does not give later leaders unlimited diplomatic, imperial, or coercive authority; it cannot support forced conversion or control over other communities.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 5:18-20","2 Corinthians 5:18-21","2 Corinthians 5:20","2 Corinthians 5:7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_8717c64d3c7ada542f345318"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Paul’s statement in verse 12 shows that his apostolic reputation was contested: he is not simply placing himself beyond evaluation. The later reconciliation appeal is attributed to God in Messiah, while people are entrusted with a message and ministry. That distinction matters when applying the passage to conflict: forgiveness, restored trust, restitution, office, access, and renewed contact are different questions and may not follow the same timetable.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 5:12","2 Corinthians 5:14-21","2 Corinthians 5:18-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_60876173827d1105a2f071df"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Hope and accountability","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The connection between future hope and judgment is important: Paul moves from longing for life with the Lord to the aim of pleasing him and to accountability for embodied deeds. This keeps hope from becoming an escape from responsibility, while also resisting the use of judgment as a measure of bodily productivity or as a tool of terror controlled by leaders.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 5:1-5","2 Corinthians 5:6-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_05cc380a176384d93c244079"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"The reconciliation appeal is framed by judgment, fear of the Lord, persuasion, known character, Messiah’s love, new creation, and an entrusted message. This wider frame prevents reconciliation language from making leaders unreviewable or from being used to compel unsafe contact or confidentiality.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 5:10-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_2e319613fd7aba717aefcc4f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The preceding argument","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"The preceding contrast between the outer and inner person leads into this chapter’s longing for heavenly dwelling, reluctance to be unclothed, concern for embodiment, and accountability. It should not be read as treating matter or the body as evil or expendable; the argument also includes embodied hope and judgment.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 4:16-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_1b4aaf643aebda991797802e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_9","text":"The wider letter is preserved in several early manuscript witnesses: P46 contains most of 2 Corinthians, P99 preserves portions, and major codices provide further witnesses with distinct textual profiles.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 10:1-18","2 Corinthians 11:1-33","2 Corinthians 12:1-21","2 Corinthians 13:1-14","2 Corinthians 1:1-24","2 Corinthians 2:1-17","2 Corinthians 3:1-18","2 Corinthians 4:1-18","2 Corinthians 6:1-18","2 Corinthians 7:1-16","2 Corinthians 8:1-24","2 Corinthians 9:1-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_005_c45b279ca4334cdb031ea3bf"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}